--TEST--
Every `Core\Script\ExitReport` member answers the same thing on a second call, and both hooks of one queue are handed the one report object with the one live error on it — `rule:observability/script-on-exit` and `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::reason
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::status
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::error
// The *agreement* shape, over the rule `rule:observability/script-on-exit` states once for the whole
// class: the report **observes** the ending rather than changing it, and the
// ending is one fact fixed before the first hook ran. So each of the three
// members reads a slot that was filled once, none of them recomputes anything,
// and every hook in the queue reads the same slots of the same object.
//
// What that rules out is a report a hook could consume or move the engine on
// with: a `status` recomputed from wherever the process had got to by the time
// a hook asked, an `error` handed on as a copy per reader, a report rebuilt for
// each hook so that two hooks could disagree about one ending. A case that asks
// once, from one hook, sees none of it — which is every other case in this
// class.
//
// The ending is an uncaught throw because it is the only one of § 2's three
// rows whose `error` is not null, and agreement about `null` is agreement two
// broken members would also reach.

// Both sets are captured by the two hooks below. Capture is by value (`rule:types/implicit-capture`
// ), and an object handle copied is still the one object — which is what
// lets the second hook count what the first one saw. The comparison is
// `Core\ObjectSet`'s own, and for an object that is identity (`rule:expressions/equality-semantics`),
// so these counts are claims about *which* object each reading was and not
// about what it said.
Core\ObjectSet<mixed> $reports = new Core\ObjectSet<mixed>();
Core\ObjectSet<mixed> $errors = new Core\ObjectSet<mixed>();
Core\ObjectSet<mixed> $statuses = new Core\ObjectSet<mixed>();

Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    $reports->add($report);

    // Each member twice with nothing in between, which is the only way a member
    // that answered off something other than its slot is observable at all.
    var $reason = $report->reason();
    var $reasonAgain = $report->reason();
    int $status = $report->status();
    int $statusAgain = $report->status();
    ?Throwable $error = $report->error();
    ?Throwable $errorAgain = $report->error();
    $errors->add($error);
    $errors->add($errorAgain);
    $statuses->add($status);

    int $agreed = 0;
    if ($reason == $reasonAgain) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    if ($status == $statusAgain) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    // `error` agrees by *identity*: § 2's third column is the live `Throwable`
    // rather than a report built from it, so the two readings collapse into one
    // member. A member that handed each caller its own copy would print the
    // same message on both lines and fail here.
    if ($errors->count() == 1) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }

    string $named = match ($reason) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    // The first answer is pinned as well as compared: two identical wrong
    // answers agree exactly as well as two right ones, and a `status` that was
    // always 0 would be perfectly self-consistent.
    string $message = "no error";
    if ($error != null) {
        $message = $error->message;
    }
    echo "first: ", $named, " ", $status, " ", $message, "\n";
    echo "first agreed: ", $agreed, " of 3\n";
});

Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    // The second hook of the same queue, reading the same three members. It
    // adds to the same three sets, so every count below is a claim about both
    // hooks rather than about this one.
    $reports->add($report);
    $errors->add($report->error());
    $statuses->add($report->status());

    string $named = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    echo "second: ", $named, " ", $report->status(), "\n";

    // One report, one error object and one status across five readings from two
    // hooks. `reports=2` would be a report built per hook; `errors=2` a copied
    // `Throwable`; `statuses=2` a status the second hook was told something
    // else about — and each of those is a queue whose hooks disagree about the
    // one ending they were all called for.
    echo "reports=", $reports->count(), " errors=", $errors->count(),
        " statuses=", $statuses->count(), "\n";
});

try {
    echo "about to fail\n";
    throw new RuntimeError("the store said no");
} finally {
    echo "the finally ran\n";
}
--EXPECT--
about to fail
the finally ran
first: UncaughtThrow 1 the store said no
first agreed: 3 of 3
second: UncaughtThrow 1
reports=1 errors=1 statuses=1
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
%A
